WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.635
-Our next guest is
an Emmy-nominated writer

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:05.839
and director behind films
such as "Bridesmaids" and "Spy"

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:08.107
as well as shows like "Freaks
and Geeks" and "The Office."

00:00:08.107 --> 00:00:11.043
He directed, produced, and wrote
"Welcome to Flatch,"

00:00:11.043 --> 00:00:12.479
which premiered today on Fox.

00:00:12.479 --> 00:00:14.013
And he also produced "Minx,"

00:00:14.013 --> 00:00:16.683
which also premiered today
on HBO Max.

00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:17.984
Let's take a look.

00:00:17.984 --> 00:00:19.452
-Like, when you give
a pill to a dog,

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:21.320
you dip it in peanut butter
first?

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:24.958
So my question is,
what's our peanut butter?

00:00:28.494 --> 00:00:30.597
-I'm sorry, are you waiting --
-It's nude men.

00:00:30.597 --> 00:00:31.730
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:32.999
Did you say nude men?

00:00:32.999 --> 00:00:35.267
-Not like a shvantz
right in the face, you know?

00:00:35.267 --> 00:00:37.936
Classy, with your modern,
you know, lady point of view.

00:00:37.936 --> 00:00:39.305
-Are you mentally ill?

00:00:39.305 --> 00:00:41.341
-You're the one who came up with
the idea.

00:00:41.341 --> 00:00:44.377
-Yeah, that was a conversation
of bon mot.

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:47.113
-Please welcome to the show
Paul Feig, everybody!

00:00:47.113 --> 00:00:49.315
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:57.924 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:57.924 --> 00:01:06.565 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:06.565 --> 00:01:09.868
Paul, it is wonderful to see
you, and you have brought us

00:01:09.868 --> 00:01:11.871
martinis to celebrate two shows
on the air today.

00:01:11.871 --> 00:01:13.273
-There you go.
I got these out of

00:01:13.273 --> 00:01:15.675
Jeanine Pirro's dressing room,
so, yeah, exactly.

00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:17.476
-I hope you left something.

00:01:17.476 --> 00:01:19.779
Leave a little money 'cause she
will hunt you down.

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:24.183
Actually, during the pandemic,
you made 100 cocktails, 1 a day.

00:01:24.183 --> 00:01:25.518
-Yeah.
-Live-streamed.

00:01:25.518 --> 00:01:27.019
-Yep.
-Which is a lovely thing to do.

00:01:27.019 --> 00:01:29.155
Did you at some point, though,
get a little tired of doing it?

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:31.957
-Never. Never making cocktails.
No, but I vowed at the beginning

00:01:31.957 --> 00:01:34.027
of the pandemic, I knew I wasn't
a medical professional,

00:01:34.027 --> 00:01:35.228
so I had to do something,
so I thought,

00:01:35.228 --> 00:01:37.297
"I'll make cocktails every day,
5:00,

00:01:37.297 --> 00:01:38.731
for 100 days in a row,
no stopping."

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:40.399
And we raised money for charity
and all that

00:01:40.399 --> 00:01:42.034
and made delicious cocktails
every day.

00:01:42.034 --> 00:01:43.636
-Well, you're very good at it.
It's an excellent martini.

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:45.872
-Thank you so much -- With my
own gin, my Artingstall's gin.

00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:47.273
I have my own gin.

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:49.476
-Well, you dress like a man
who should have his own gin.

00:01:49.476 --> 00:01:51.043
-There you go.
Thank you, thank you.

00:01:51.043 --> 00:01:53.146
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:53.146 --> 00:01:55.515
-So I want to talk about "Minx"
first. That was the clip.

00:01:55.515 --> 00:01:57.683
-Yeah.
-So, this is about the sort of

00:01:57.683 --> 00:02:00.186
launching a --
I guess you'd call it --

00:02:00.186 --> 00:02:02.388
What would you call this
magazine, a woman's magazine?

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:05.725
-Well, it's kind of a fictional
retelling of the story of

00:02:05.725 --> 00:02:07.493
how "Playgirl" magazine
got started

00:02:07.493 --> 00:02:09.863
but with all new characters
and everything.

00:02:09.863 --> 00:02:12.031
Ellen Rapoport created the show.

00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:14.567
And, you know, Jake Johnson
is so funny.

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:16.502
It's literally the role
he was born to play.

00:02:16.502 --> 00:02:18.337
And Ophelia Lovibond.

00:02:18.337 --> 00:02:20.773
And it's just a really fun,
funny show.

00:02:20.773 --> 00:02:22.308
Takes place in 1971.

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:24.043
Lots of laughs
and tons of male nudity.

00:02:24.043 --> 00:02:25.879
-Tons of male nudity?
-Tons of male nudity.

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:29.716
-I should note, male nudity
presented very matter-of-fact.

00:02:29.716 --> 00:02:31.451
I guess the way it would be

00:02:31.451 --> 00:02:33.219
if you were about to put it
in a magazine.

00:02:33.219 --> 00:02:34.320
-Well, that's the thing.
We didn't want the show

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.255
to be lurid,
so it's about this --

00:02:36.255 --> 00:02:38.491
Basically,
Jake plays the publisher

00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:40.926
of a lot of naked magazines,
but for women.

00:02:40.926 --> 00:02:43.229
So when you get there the first
time and she, you know,

00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:46.399
our star tours the place, it's
just a bunch of people naked,

00:02:46.399 --> 00:02:47.933
standing around,
smoking cigarettes,

00:02:47.933 --> 00:02:50.769
waiting to do their job,
so it's immediately not sexy.

00:02:50.769 --> 00:02:52.438
It's just like
a workplace comedy.

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:53.640
-There you go.
-There you go.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:56.041
With lots of dongs.
-Lots of dongs.

00:02:56.041 --> 00:02:57.776
It's like "The Office"
with dongs.

00:02:57.776 --> 00:02:59.011
[ Laughter ]
-That's the pitch.

00:02:59.011 --> 00:03:00.479
-That's the pitch.
But what was it like

00:03:00.479 --> 00:03:02.981
when you went and pitched?
-Well, we drove around.

00:03:02.981 --> 00:03:06.351
Ellen had a trunkful of
old "Playgirl" magazines,

00:03:06.351 --> 00:03:08.788
and we would show up with
a giant pile, spread them out.

00:03:08.788 --> 00:03:10.690
Everybody loved to see those.
Everybody had a great time.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:12.324
And we'd have a great pitch.

00:03:12.324 --> 00:03:13.693
And then we'd leave and go like,
"Well, they bought it."

00:03:13.693 --> 00:03:15.294
And then you get a call,
"Well, they don't want to do it"

00:03:15.294 --> 00:03:16.429
'cause it's too whatever.

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:17.497
-"But they will buy
the magazines."

00:03:17.497 --> 00:03:19.865
-"They will buy the magazines,"
exactly.

00:03:19.865 --> 00:03:23.469
But eventually, HBO Max stepped
up, and we got to make the show.

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:25.604
And it was just so much fun.

00:03:25.604 --> 00:03:28.040
-So you moved out to L.A.
in the '80s?

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.777
-Yeah, '81, to be a tour guide
at Universal Studios.

00:03:30.777 --> 00:03:32.144
-And how were you
as a tour guide?

00:03:32.144 --> 00:03:33.679
-I fell in Jaws Lake.

00:03:33.679 --> 00:03:35.782
-Did you really?
-Yes, I did. Yeah.

00:03:35.782 --> 00:03:38.785
If you've ever been on the tour,
they have Jaws Lake,

00:03:38.785 --> 00:03:41.254
and you go out on this dock,
and then it, like, tips,

00:03:41.254 --> 00:03:43.288
and then the shark comes out
and does all this stuff.

00:03:43.288 --> 00:03:46.024
Well, this is '81.
So, some lady was wearing clogs,

00:03:46.024 --> 00:03:48.827
and the thing tipped
and her clog fell in the water.

00:03:48.827 --> 00:03:50.663
And so we come out,
and she goes, "My clog --"

00:03:50.663 --> 00:03:52.398
So I go, "Oh."
So, I clamber back out

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:54.700
onto this dock,
and I'm reaching out to get it.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:57.669
And the dock resets, and I go
headfirst into the water

00:03:57.669 --> 00:04:00.006
as Jaws is backing up to reset.

00:04:00.006 --> 00:04:02.475
So I almost got killed by Jaws
in reverse.

00:04:02.475 --> 00:04:03.976
[ Laughter ]

00:04:03.976 --> 00:04:06.812
-Getting killed by a fake Jaws
would be somehow less dramatic.

00:04:06.812 --> 00:04:08.214
-Yeah, that's just
a funny death,

00:04:08.214 --> 00:04:09.548
but I don't want to have
a funny death.

00:04:09.548 --> 00:04:10.817
That's all I ask.

00:04:10.817 --> 00:04:14.754
-So, "Flatch" is an adaptation
of a British show.

00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:16.755
And you also worked on
the American "Office."

00:04:16.755 --> 00:04:19.191
-Yeah.
-And so was that helpful?

00:04:19.191 --> 00:04:21.661
Like, going through that process
again and taking

00:04:21.661 --> 00:04:23.696
a beloved British property
and making it --

00:04:23.696 --> 00:04:25.431
adapting it to
the United States?

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:27.133
-Yeah, it was, yeah,
because "Flatch" is based on

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:29.167
a show called "This Country,"
which was on BBC.

00:04:29.167 --> 00:04:32.472
And yeah, what we found out
on "The Office" is that

00:04:32.472 --> 00:04:36.542
American audiences don't love
rooting for terrible people

00:04:36.542 --> 00:04:38.678
as the lead character.
-Yeah.

00:04:38.678 --> 00:04:43.082
-And so, like, on that,
the first six episodes we did

00:04:43.082 --> 00:04:45.852
on "The Office," he was mean,
but they didn't like it,

00:04:45.852 --> 00:04:47.152
and then "The 40-Year-Old
Virgin" came out,

00:04:47.152 --> 00:04:49.121
and people -- "Oh, he's nice."
So then we made him

00:04:49.121 --> 00:04:50.489
more well-weaning.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:50.489 --> 00:04:51.924
And this one is just great.
It's just about these

00:04:51.924 --> 00:04:54.460
two cousins who live in this
small town in the middle of Ohio

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:55.728
and just about the people
in the town.

00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:58.664
It's a lovely, super fun,
comfort-food kind of show.

00:04:58.664 --> 00:05:01.200
-And you pulled from your own
small-town experiences

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.735
with "Freaks and Geeks," so is
that nice to return to that?

00:05:03.735 --> 00:05:05.738
-Yeah, it's really fun.
It's fun to write scripts --

00:05:05.738 --> 00:05:07.606
'cause basically these two
characters are so kind of

00:05:07.606 --> 00:05:10.743
"Arrested Development" that
I can just bring up stories

00:05:10.743 --> 00:05:13.145
that I did when I was a kid,
and one of the things I wrote

00:05:13.145 --> 00:05:15.180
that's an episode is they start
a dance school.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:18.717
And I did that. I started
a dance school in my garage

00:05:18.717 --> 00:05:20.986
and charged all the kids
in the neighborhood a dollar.

00:05:20.986 --> 00:05:22.988
And this was the dance
I invented.

00:05:22.988 --> 00:05:24.123
May I?
-Oh, great.

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:28.527
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:28.527 --> 00:05:31.063
-Manage your expectations
because here it comes.

00:05:31.063 --> 00:05:34.633
[ Laughter ]

00:05:34.633 --> 00:05:39.638
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:39.638 --> 00:05:41.040
There you go.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:46.145
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:46.145 --> 00:05:49.348
And within five minutes
of the class ending,

00:05:49.348 --> 00:05:51.016
a bunch of older brothers
came over and threatened

00:05:51.016 --> 00:05:52.318
to beat me up if I didn't
give their money back.

00:05:52.318 --> 00:05:53.819
-I was gonna say I'm surprised

00:05:53.819 --> 00:05:55.521
there hasn't been
a class action.

00:05:55.521 --> 00:05:57.055
When you first went to L.A.,

00:05:57.055 --> 00:05:59.291
you thought you'd be in front of
the camera, right?

00:05:59.291 --> 00:06:01.527
-Yeah, I went there to be
an actor, and I was an actor.

00:06:01.527 --> 00:06:04.096
I was on a lot of -- I was
a regular on five TV shows,

00:06:04.096 --> 00:06:06.966
and I was Mr. Pool
on "Sabrina the Teenage Witch"

00:06:06.966 --> 00:06:08.334
for the first season.
-Wow.

00:06:08.334 --> 00:06:10.403
-Yes, "mitosis is,"
if you remember that.

00:06:10.403 --> 00:06:13.038
-And so when did the moment --
When did it dawn on you

00:06:13.038 --> 00:06:16.141
that your future path
in Hollywood

00:06:16.141 --> 00:06:17.343
was gonna be behind the camera?

00:06:17.343 --> 00:06:18.844
-Well, I always wanted to write
and direct.

00:06:18.844 --> 00:06:21.246
And when we finished
the first season of "Sabrina,"

00:06:21.246 --> 00:06:22.415
I was like, "Oh, my God."

00:06:22.415 --> 00:06:23.983
It was a hit show,
so, like, I made money.

00:06:23.983 --> 00:06:25.384
So I was like, "I'm gonna take
all my money and sink it

00:06:25.384 --> 00:06:27.386
into this independent film
that I wrote and directed

00:06:27.386 --> 00:06:29.088
and starred in
that I shot in six days"

00:06:29.088 --> 00:06:31.691
and finished that with all
the money I had in the world.

00:06:31.691 --> 00:06:33.025
And then they called me up
and said,

00:06:33.025 --> 00:06:34.660
"Oh, we're not gonna bring you
back to the show."

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:37.663
So then I was out of work
and out of money.

00:06:37.663 --> 00:06:40.099
My wife was looking at me like,
"What did you just do?"

00:06:40.099 --> 00:06:42.234
And I had a terrible year of
just trying to sell that movie.

00:06:42.234 --> 00:06:44.036
Couldn't get it anywhere,
but during that time,

00:06:44.036 --> 00:06:46.472
then I wrote the script
for "Freaks and Geeks."

00:06:46.472 --> 00:06:48.240
-Well, it certainly I think
ended up

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:49.609
being the perfect path for you.

00:06:49.609 --> 00:06:51.076
And it's so nice
to have you here.

00:06:51.076 --> 00:06:53.079
Congratulations on two shows.
-Thank you, Seth.

00:06:53.079 --> 00:06:55.280
-You guys, that's Paul Feig.

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:58.050
"Welcome to Flatch" airs
Thursdays on Fox,

00:06:58.050 --> 00:06:59.852
and "Minx" is streaming on
HBO Max.

00:06:59.852 --> 00:07:01.854
We'll be right back with
more "Late Night," everybody.

